@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@code{mab-org.el} bundles the helpers used to assemble manuscript-style
modular annotated bibliographies (mab) and lighter-weight annotated
bibliographies (abib) inside Org-mode buffers.

The package wraps citar (and ebib) citekeys with LaTeX
@code{\bibentry} code, creates per-citekey Org notes from a
configurable template, optionally appends BibTeX entries to a
project-specific @file{.bib} file, and bulk applies the same wrapping
to every citation in a region.  It also stamps out a fresh
@file{mab<NUMBER>/mab<NUMBER>.org} project file from a built-in
template, finds or creates such a file by project number, and appends
a wrapped block to the project file directly from inside @code{ebib}.

@cindex annotated bibliography
@cindex bibentry
@cindex citar
@cindex ebib
@cindex Org-mode

When a per-citekey note already exists (for example because it was
created during work on another project), the package detects the
duplicate and offers to reuse the existing note, create a variant
with the next available letter suffix, or quit without changing the
buffer.

@node Quick Start
@chapter Quick Start

Once the package is on your @code{load-path} and @code{(require 'mab-org)}
has been evaluated, the three most common entry points are:

@table @kbd
@item M-x mab-org-create-project
Stamp out a new @file{mab<NUMBER>/mab<NUMBER>.org} project file from
the built-in template.

@item M-x mab-org-wrap-citekey
With point on a citekey or @code{[cite:@@KEY]} in any buffer, replace
the citekey with the wrapped LaTeX block, create the per-citekey
note, and open it.

@item M-x mab-org-add-bib-item
From the @code{ebib} index buffer, append the wrapped block for the
current entry to the configured mab project file.  Bound to @kbd{B}
in @code{ebib-index-mode-map} once @code{ebib} is loaded.
@end table

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@section Manual

Put the directory on your @code{load-path} and load the package.

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/mab-org/")
(require 'mab-org)
@end lisp

@section With @code{use-package}

@lisp
(use-package mab-org
  :load-path "/path/to/mab-org/"
  :commands (mab-org-create-project
             mab-org-open-file
             mab-org-wrap-citekey
             mab-org-wrap-citekey-abib
             mab-org-wrap-region
             mab-org-insert-matching-keys
             mab-org-add-bib-item))
@end lisp

@section From a system @code{make install}

@xref{Makefile Targets}, for a description of @code{make install} and
@code{make install-info}.  Both honor a @code{PREFIX} variable so the
files can be staged anywhere under your home directory.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

The package exposes user options through the customization group
@code{mab-org}.  Customize them with @kbd{M-x customize-group RET
mab-org RET} or set them in your init file.

@defopt mab-org-notes-directory
@vindex mab-org-notes-directory
Directory that holds per-citekey Org notes.  Default
@file{~/abibNotes/}.
@end defopt

@defopt mab-org-pdfs-directory
@vindex mab-org-pdfs-directory
Directory that holds renamed article PDFs.  Default
@file{~/0papersLabeled/}.
@end defopt

@defopt mab-org-books-directory
@vindex mab-org-books-directory
Directory that holds renamed book PDFs.  A second file link is added
to every wrapped block so notes derived from books are reachable
without changing the template.  Default @file{~/0booksLabeled/}.
@end defopt

@defopt mab-org-global-bib-file
@vindex mab-org-global-bib-file
Path to the global BibTeX library, searched by
@code{mab-org-insert-matching-keys}.  Default
@file{~/Documents/global.bib}.
@end defopt

@defopt mab-org-region-confirm-threshold
@vindex mab-org-region-confirm-threshold
Maximum number of citations a region may carry before
@code{mab-org-wrap-region} asks for confirmation.  Default 10.
@end defopt

@defopt mab-org-project-number-in-note-stem
@vindex mab-org-project-number-in-note-stem
How to incorporate the project number into the abibnote
filename stem.  One of:

@table @code
@item nil
Never include the project number; the stem is the citekey, and
variants use a single-letter suffix.  This is the existing
behavior.

@item t
Always include the project number; the stem is
@code{<citekey><sep><project-number>}, and letter variants are
applied on top of that base.  When the project number is empty
the package falls back to the bare citekey and emits a message.

@item ask
Include the project number on demand.  The default stem is the
citekey.  When a note already exists for that citekey, the
variant prompt offers a project-suffixed stem alongside the
letter-suffixed stem.  Pick the project variant to fork the
note for the current project.
@end table

The default is @code{nil}.
@end defopt

@defopt mab-org-project-number-separator
@vindex mab-org-project-number-separator
Separator placed between the citekey and the project number.
Used only when the resolved stem includes the project number.
The default is a hyphen.  An empty string is allowed but is a
poor choice for citekeys that already end in digits because, for
example, @code{Coppens1997} followed by @code{2156} would become
the ambiguous @code{Coppens19972156}.
@end defopt

@defopt mab-org-book-entry-regexp
@vindex mab-org-book-entry-regexp
Regexp identifying a BibTeX entry that should use the book PDF
link instead of the article PDF link.  Matched against the raw
entry text (case-insensitively).  The default is
@code{"\\`[[:space:]]*@@book"}, which catches @code{@@book} and
@code{@@booklet} through prefix match.  Extend it to also catch
@code{@@inbook} or @code{@@incollection} when needed.
@end defopt

@defopt mab-org-base-directory
@vindex mab-org-base-directory
Default base directory under which mab project files live.  When
nil, @code{mab-org-create-project} falls back to the directory of
the current buffer (or @code{default-directory} when there is no
buffer file).
@end defopt

@defopt mab-org-mab-path
@vindex mab-org-mab-path
Default destination for @code{mab-org-add-bib-item}.  This is the
absolute path of the mab project Org file to which ebib entries are
appended.  When nil, the user is prompted with no default.  The
variable is updated automatically when @code{mab-org-add-bib-item}
is called interactively, so the most recent choice persists for the
rest of the session.
@end defopt

@node Public Commands
@chapter Public Commands

This chapter documents every interactive command in the package.

@deffn Command mab-org-create-project project-number project-tag project-title @
       &optional base-dir bib-path
@findex mab-org-create-project
Stamp out a new project at
@file{BASE/mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>/mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>.org}.

@var{project-number} is the numeric project identifier (for example
@code{"0386"}).  @var{project-tag} is the short tag that follows the
number in the document title.  @var{project-title} is the longer
human-readable title used in the running header.  @var{bib-path} is
the path passed to LaTeX @code{\bibliography}; when nil,
@code{mab-org-global-bib-file} is used (with the @file{.bib} suffix
stripped).

The function refuses to overwrite an existing project file.

Interactive synopsis:

@example
M-x mab-org-create-project RET 0386 RET mpQcr RET multipass QCr RET
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Command mab-org-open-file project-number &optional base-dir
@findex mab-org-open-file
Find or create a mab project file by @var{project-number}.  When
@file{BASE/mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>/mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>.org} already
exists it is opened.  Otherwise the user is asked whether to create
it from the template, and if so prompted for a project tag and
title.
@end deffn

@deffn Command mab-org-wrap-citekey
@findex mab-org-wrap-citekey
Replace the citekey under the cursor with LaTeX-wrapped text and
create an empty per-citekey Org note when one does not yet exist.
Works with @code{[cite:@@KEY]}, plain LaTeX style, or bare-word
citekeys.  The wrapped block contains @code{\bibentry@{KEY@}},
@code{\addcontentsline@{toc@}@{subsubsection@}}, an
@code{#+INCLUDE:} directive, a bare file link to the per-note
Org file, and a single PDF link.  The PDF link points at
@code{mab-org-pdfs-directory} by default, or at
@code{mab-org-books-directory} when the BibTeX entry for the
citekey matches @code{mab-org-book-entry-regexp}.  The links
are not wrapped in a Notes drawer, so they remain visible in
the exported document.

When a note for the citekey already exists, the user is prompted
to (r)euse, create a (v)ariant, or (q)uit.  @xref{Variant
Resolution}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command mab-org-wrap-citekey-abib
@findex mab-org-wrap-citekey-abib
Same as @code{mab-org-wrap-citekey}, with two differences.  First,
the project @file{.bib} file is written next to the current buffer
(as @file{ab<NUMBER>.bib}) rather than in a @file{mab<NUMBER>/}
subdirectory.  Second, the inserted block keeps the same two
file links (the per-note Org file and the chosen article or
book PDF) inside an Org @code{BEGIN_COMMENT/END_COMMENT} block,
so they do not appear in the exported document.
@end deffn

@deffn Command mab-org-wrap-region start end
@findex mab-org-wrap-region
Apply @code{mab-org-wrap-citekey} to every citation in the active
region.  Citations are processed from end to beginning so that
earlier positions remain valid as later citations expand.  The
command asks for confirmation when the region holds more than
@code{mab-org-region-confirm-threshold} citations.
@end deffn

@deffn Command mab-org-insert-matching-keys term
@findex mab-org-insert-matching-keys
Read a search @var{term}, scan @code{mab-org-global-bib-file}, and
insert one @code{[cite:@@CITEKEY]} line at point for every BibTeX
entry whose raw text contains the term (case insensitive).
Returns the list of citekeys, so the function is also useful from
other Lisp.
@end deffn

@deffn Command mab-org-add-bib-item &optional file-path
@findex mab-org-add-bib-item
When called from @code{ebib}'s index buffer, append the wrapped
LaTeX block for the current entry to a mab project Org file.  The
wrapped block lands under the section @code{\section*@{Illustrated
and annotated bibliography@}}, before any
@code{\section*@{Backmatter@}} section if one is present, or at the
end of the file otherwise.  The per-citekey abibnote is also
created when it does not already exist.

The destination defaults to @code{mab-org-mab-path}; the most
recent choice is remembered for the rest of the session.

When @code{ebib} is loaded, the command is bound to @kbd{B} in
@code{ebib-index-mode-map}.
@end deffn

@node Project Template
@chapter Project Template

The built-in project template lives inside @file{mab-org.el} as the
constant @code{mab-org--template-string}.  A reference copy ships in
@file{mabXXXX.org} so you can preview the output before generating a
new project file.

@code{mab-org-create-project} performs five substitutions in the
template:

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem Placeholder @tab Source
@item Document title
@tab Project number plus project tag
@item Running header
@tab Project number plus project full title
@item Sub-directory
@tab @file{mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>/}
@item File name
@tab @file{mab<PROJECT-NUMBER>.org}
@item @code{\bibliography@{@}}
@tab @code{mab-org-global-bib-file} without the @file{.bib} suffix
@end multitable

@node Variant Resolution
@chapter Variant Resolution

@cindex variant note
@cindex duplicate note

When @code{mab-org-wrap-citekey} or @code{mab-org-wrap-citekey-abib}
detects an existing @file{<citekey>.org} in
@code{mab-org-notes-directory}, it asks the user how to proceed:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{r} (reuse)
Keep the existing note.  No new file is written; the wrapped block
references the existing note.

@item @kbd{v} (variant)
Create a new note whose stem has the next available lowercase letter
appended.  For example, if @file{Foo2020Bar.org} already exists, the
new stem becomes @file{Foo2020Bara}; if @file{Foo2020Bara.org} also
exists, the next stem is @file{Foo2020Barb}.

@item @kbd{q} (quit)
Abort without changing the buffer.  No file is written, no text is
inserted.
@end table

What the variant suffix changes:

@itemize @bullet
@item
The @code{\addcontentsline} table-of-contents entry uses the stem,
so a variant gets a distinct row in the TOC.
@item
The @code{#+INCLUDE:} directive and the Org-note link use the stem,
so the variant note is the file actually opened and included.
@item
The @code{\bibentry} command and the article and book PDF links keep
the original citekey, because the BibTeX library and the PDF/book
archives are keyed by citekey.
@end itemize

@node Project-Number Suffix
@chapter Project-Number Suffix

@cindex project number
@cindex per-project notes

The package can optionally append the current project number to the
abibnote filename stem.  Three modes are available, controlled by
@code{mab-org-project-number-in-note-stem}.

@section Mode @code{nil} (default)

The stem is the citekey.  Variants use a single-letter suffix.

@example
~/abibNotes/Coppens1997Xray.org
~/abibNotes/Coppens1997Xraya.org    @r{(letter variant)}
@end example

@section Mode @code{t}

The stem is @code{<citekey><sep><project-number>}.  Letter variants
apply on top of that base.  This mode is appropriate when each
project gets its own copy of every note.

@example
~/abibNotes/Coppens1997Xray-2156.org
~/abibNotes/Coppens1997Xray-3010.org
~/abibNotes/Coppens1997Xray-2156a.org   @r{(letter variant within 2156)}
@end example

When the project number is empty (no value entered at the wrap
prompt, or no digit-only stem in the destination file name), the
package falls back to the bare citekey and emits a message.  Nothing
aborts.

@section Mode @code{ask}

The default stem is the citekey, and the variant prompt offers a
project-suffixed stem alongside the letter-suffixed stem.  This is
the most flexible mode.  The user shares notes across projects most
of the time and forks a project-specific copy on demand.

The variant prompt for an existing
@file{Coppens1997Xray.org} in mode @code{ask} with project
@code{2156} reads:

@example
Note Coppens1997Xray.org already exists
  r  reuse              Reuse the existing note for this citekey
  v  variant            Create a variant note as Coppens1997Xraya.org
  p  project-variant    Create a project-specific variant as
                        Coppens1997Xray-2156.org
  q  quit               Abort the wrap operation
@end example

The @code{p} option is suppressed when no project number is
available, when @code{Coppens1997Xray-2156.org} already exists, and
when the project-suffixed stem would collide with the base stem.

@section Migrating between modes

Switching from @code{nil} to @code{t} does not rename existing
notes.  New entries land at the project-suffixed path while old
shared notes remain at the bare-citekey path.  To merge the two
layouts, copy or symlink the bare-citekey notes into project-suffixed
copies in the shell.

@node Ebib Integration
@chapter Ebib Integration

The package integrates with @code{ebib} via
@code{mab-org-add-bib-item}.  The command must be invoked from an
@code{ebib} index buffer; outside it the command signals
@code{user-error}.

Once @code{ebib} is loaded, the package binds the command to @kbd{B}:

@lisp
(eval-after-load 'ebib
  '(when (boundp 'ebib-index-mode-map)
     (define-key ebib-index-mode-map (kbd "B") #'mab-org-add-bib-item)))
@end lisp

The flow:

@enumerate
@item
Open ebib (@kbd{M-x ebib}) and navigate to the entry you want to
annotate.

@item
Press @kbd{B}.

@item
Confirm the destination mab project file (default
@code{mab-org-mab-path}, if set, otherwise prompt with no default).

@item
The package writes the wrapped block under the section
@code{\section*@{Illustrated and annotated bibliography@}},
appended just below the last existing entry and above any
@code{\clearpage} (and surrounding blank lines) that introduces
the @code{\section*@{Backmatter@}} heading, so the entry stays
inside the bibliography section in the rendered document.  An
empty per-citekey abibnote is created if none exists yet.
@end enumerate

@node The Test Suite
@chapter The Test Suite

The companion file @file{mab-org-test.el} carries an ERT regression
suite for the package.

@section What the suite covers

@itemize @bullet
@item
Citekey extraction from the three accepted forms
(@code{[cite:@@KEY]}, @code{[cite:@@a,@@b]}, bare word, nil).

@item
Default project number detection from filenames
(@file{mab<N>.org}, @file{ab<N>.org}, bare digits, no digits, nil).

@item
mab-style and abib-style wrapped text rendering, including the
default-to-article behavior, the @code{book-p} switch to the
books directory, and the variant-stem split that keeps the
bibentry and PDF link on the citekey while INCLUDE/TOC/note
link follow the stem.

@item
@code{mab-org--book-entry-p} predicate, including the default
@code{@@book} prefix match and a custom regexp that adds
@code{@@inbook} and @code{@@incollection}.

@item
@code{mab-org--lookup-bibtex-entry} fallback to
@code{mab-org-global-bib-file} when citar is not loaded.

@item
Region citation collection and ordering.

@item
Empty-note creation, including missing-directory creation, and
not overwriting an existing note.

@item
Variant resolution: no existing note (no prompt), reuse, variant,
all letters taken (variant option suppressed), quit (signals
@code{user-error}).

@item
Project-number suffix in all three modes.  Mode @code{nil}
ignores the project number.  Mode @code{t} returns
@code{CITEKEY-PROJECT}, falls back to the bare citekey when the
project number is empty, and walks letters on top of the
project-suffixed base.  Mode @code{ask} suppresses the
project-variant choice when no project number is available, when
the project target file already exists, and when the suffixed
stem would equal the base stem.

@item
Project template substitution and project file creation, including
the @file{mab<N>/} subdirectory layout, refusal to overwrite, and
nested base directory creation.

@item
BibTeX append helper (creates new file, preserves existing
content).

@item
Bounds detection (citation block versus bare word at point).

@item
The search-and-insert command against an in-memory @file{global.bib}.

@item
@code{mab-org-add-bib-item} insertion behavior with and without a
Backmatter section, with the new entry placed above any
@code{\clearpage} that introduces Backmatter, the
missing-section error, the rejected non-ebib buffer error, and
automatic note creation.

@item
Public command surface: every command listed in @ref{Public
Commands} is bound and interactive.
@end itemize

@section Helpers used by the suite

@deffn Function mab-org-test--with-temp-dir fn
@findex mab-org-test--with-temp-dir
Call @var{fn} with the path to a fresh temporary directory.  The
directory and its contents are deleted after @var{fn} returns.
@end deffn

@deffn Macro mab-org-test-with-temp-dir var &rest body
@findex mab-org-test-with-temp-dir
Bind @var{var} to a fresh temporary directory and evaluate
@var{body}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function mab-org-test--read-file path
@findex mab-org-test--read-file
Return the contents of @var{path} as a string.
@end deffn

@deffn Function mab-org-test--make-mab-file path &optional with-backmatter
@findex mab-org-test--make-mab-file
Write a minimal mab Org file to @var{path} for testing.  When
@var{with-backmatter} is non-nil, also include a Backmatter
section after the bibliography heading.
@end deffn

@section Mocking ebib and read-multiple-choice

The suite uses @code{cl-letf} to mock @code{read-multiple-choice}
for the variant-resolution tests, and @code{ebib--get-key-at-point}
together with @code{derived-mode-p} for the ebib-integration tests.
This means the suite runs without @code{ebib} or @code{citar}
installed.

@section Running the suite

@example
make test
@end example

The Makefile target removes any stale @file{.elc} before running so
Emacs cannot accidentally load an out-of-date byte-compiled
definition.  @xref{Makefile Targets}.

@node Makefile Targets
@chapter Makefile Targets

The Makefile uses GNU-style installation variables that you can
override on the command line.

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .75
@headitem Variable @tab Default
@item @code{PREFIX}
@tab @code{$(HOME)/.local}
@item @code{INFODIR}
@tab @code{$(PREFIX)/share/info}
@item @code{EMACSDIR}
@tab @code{$(PREFIX)/share/emacs/site-lisp}
@item @code{EMACS}
@tab @code{emacs}
@item @code{INSTALL}
@tab @code{install}
@item @code{INSTALL_INFO}
@tab @code{install-info}
@end multitable

@table @code
@item make all
The default target.  Equivalent to @code{make compile test}.

@item make test
Run the ERT suite in batch mode.  Removes stale @file{.elc} first so
Emacs cannot load an outdated byte-compiled definition.

@item make compile
Byte-compile @file{mab-org.el} to @file{mab-org.elc}.

@item make info
Build @file{mab-org.info} from @file{mab-org.texi} with
@code{makeinfo}.

@item make install
Install @file{mab-org.el} into @code{$(EMACSDIR)}.  Override
@code{PREFIX} to choose another location.

@item make install-info
Build @file{mab-org.info} (if necessary) and install it into
@code{$(INFODIR)}, then run @code{install-info} so the page is
registered with @code{info}'s top-level directory.

@item make uninstall
Remove the installed @file{mab-org.el} from @code{$(EMACSDIR)}.

@item make uninstall-info
Remove the installed @file{mab-org.info} from @code{$(INFODIR)} and
unregister it from the top-level directory.

@item make lint
Run @code{checkdoc} against @file{mab-org.el}.

@item make clean
Remove all build products, including @file{.elc} and @file{.info}.
@end table

A typical user-level install:

@example
make
make install
make install-info
@end example

A typical system install (with @code{sudo}):

@example
sudo make PREFIX=/usr/local install install-info
@end example

@node Compatibility
@chapter Compatibility

Tested with Emacs 27.1 and later.  @code{bibtex.el} is the only
required dependency.  @code{citar} is optional and is only used when
the wrapper commands need to copy a BibTeX entry into the project
@file{.bib} file.  @code{ebib} is optional and is only used by
@code{mab-org-add-bib-item}; the key binding registration is gated
on @code{(eval-after-load 'ebib ...)}.

@node Changelog
@chapter Changelog

@table @strong
@item 0.5.0
Added an optional project-number suffix on abibnote stems,
controlled by @code{mab-org-project-number-in-note-stem} (modes
@code{nil}, @code{t}, @code{ask}).  Mode @code{ask} adds a
project-variant choice to the existing reuse/variant/quit prompt.
Added @code{mab-org-project-number-separator} so the spelling of
the suffix is configurable.

@item 0.4.0
Replaced the two PDF lines in the wrapped block with a single
PDF link.  The link points at @code{mab-org-pdfs-directory} by
default and at @code{mab-org-books-directory} when the BibTeX
entry matches @code{mab-org-book-entry-regexp} (default:
@code{@@book} prefix).  Added @code{mab-org--lookup-bibtex-entry}
which tries citar first and falls back to
@code{mab-org-global-bib-file}, and added
@code{mab-org--book-entry-p}.

@item 0.3.2
Removed the @code{:Notes:} ... @code{:END:} drawer and the
``Add more prose. Add tables. Add figures.'' line from the
mab-style wrapped block.  The three file links (to the per-note
Org file, the article PDF, and the book PDF) are retained and now
appear as bare lines.  Reworked
@code{mab-org-add-bib-item} so the new entry is appended above
any @code{\clearpage} that introduces the Backmatter section, so
entries stay inside the bibliography section in the rendered
document.

@item 0.3.1
Removed the per-citekey note template.  New abibnotes are created
empty.  Dropped @code{mab-org-note-template} and
@code{mab-org-create-note-files}.

@item 0.3.0
Renamed the package from @code{mab} to @code{mab-org}.  Adopted the
@code{mab<NUMBER>/} subdirectory convention for project files.
Added @code{mab-org-add-bib-item} for ebib integration,
@code{mab-org-open-file} for find-or-create, and
@code{mab-org-books-directory} so wrapped blocks include a book PDF
link.

@item 0.2.0
Added the variant-note prompt (reuse, variant, quit) for duplicate
abibnotes, an alphabet-walking next-letter helper, and the
two-argument render functions that accept a stem distinct from the
citekey.

@item 0.1.0
Initial collection: wrap a citekey, wrap a region, search the
global bib, and stamp out a project file from a template.
@end table

@node MELPA Notes
@chapter MELPA Notes

@cindex MELPA

The package follows the conventions checked by
@code{package-build} and @code{package-lint}:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{lexical-binding: t} declared on the first line.
@item
Standard package header with @code{Author},
@code{Maintainer}, @code{URL}, @code{Version},
@code{Package-Requires}, and @code{Keywords}.
@item
A trailing @code{;;; mab-org.el ends here} marker.
@item
@code{(provide 'mab-org)} is the last form in the file.
@item
Every public symbol is prefixed with @code{mab-org-}; every
internal symbol is prefixed with @code{mab-org--}.
@item
@code{;;;###autoload} cookies on the public commands.
@item
@code{(require 'bibtex)} declares the only mandatory dependency
explicitly.  @code{citar} and @code{ebib} are optional and are
detected at runtime via @code{(featurep 'citar)} and
@code{(eval-after-load 'ebib ...)}.
@end itemize

This Texinfo file is built by @code{make info} and produces
@file{mab-org.info}, which carries the @code{@@dircategory Emacs}
and @code{@@direntry} required to register the page with
@code{info}'s top-level directory.  The MELPA recipe should list
the @file{.texi} (and the resulting @file{.info}) under
@code{:files} when an info-installable build is desired.

